**Goal** — Add `kodaToolDescriptorsFor(kind)` so a WORKSPACE SESSION ASSIGN carries `koda_my_projects` plus every existing koda tool rewritten with a required `project` argument, and record the tools-unavailable notice and instructions line for WORKSPACE sessions.

**Motivation** — After 1d-1 a WORKSPACE session can only brainstorm: `withKodaTools` returns the thread unchanged for `WORKSPACE` (`apps/api/src/fleet/jobs/placement.service.ts:224-227`) and its instructions say it has no koda tools (`apps/api/src/fleet/threads/thread-instructions.ts:53-59`).

**Approach** — 1d2-D1 one feature, api + web, no runner change: the runner already accepts `kodaTools` on any SESSION ASSIGN (`apps/runner/src/supervisor/assign-parser.ts:243-245`) and builds a proxy per descriptor, and the WORKSPACE catalog (13 tools) is under `KODA_TOOL_LIMITS.maxTools` (32). No protocol change; `FLEET_PROTOCOL_VERSION` stays 4.
1d2-D2 per-kind catalog. `kodaToolDescriptorsFor(kind)` answers the descriptors an ASSIGN carries. REPO and PROJECT: exactly `KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS`, unchanged. WORKSPACE: `koda_my_projects` first, then every descriptor of `KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS` in order, each rewritten by `withProjectArgument`: a `project` property (the slug pattern `^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$`, `maxLength` 100) is added to `inputSchema.properties`, `project` is prepended to `inputSchema.required`, and the description gains the sentence `In a workspace thread, name the project with \`project\` (a slug koda_my_projects lists).` The originals are never mutated.
1d2-D9 notice and instructions. A WORKSPACE session on a runner without `kodaTools` records `kodaToolsUnavailableOn` as REPO and PROJECT sessions do (the 1d-1 exception is removed in `PrismaThreadIngestRepository.sessionNotices`, `prisma-thread-ingest.repository.ts:477-489`). The WORKSPACE instructions gain a koda tools line naming the `koda_my_projects` tool and the `project` argument; the second line no longer says the session has no koda tools; they still carry no project brief and no repository context.
`withKodaTools` target: for any SESSION on a `kodaTools` runner returns `{ ...thread, kodaTools: [...kodaToolDescriptorsFor(thread.kind ?? 'REPO')] }`; unchanged otherwise.
Update existing tests: `placement.service.project-code.spec.ts:175-184` (the `not.toHaveProperty('kodaTools')` assertion becomes equality with `kodaToolDescriptorsFor('WORKSPACE')`; `projectRepos`/`findProjectRepos` assertions stay), `prisma-thread-ingest.repository.spec.ts:727-732` (becomes `kodaToolsUnavailableOn: 'box'`), `workspace-turns.spec.ts:45-50` (`not.toContain('koda_')` becomes `toContain('koda_my_projects')`; role-line and `Skills available: none.` assertions stay).

**Scope** — In: descriptors, `withKodaTools`, instructions line, ingest notice, the three listed spec updates. Out: the executor `project` handling and route (US-002), the tool implementation `myProjectsTool` and registration (US-003), any web work.

**Interface**
```ts
export const PROJECT_ARGUMENT = { type: 'string', pattern: '^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$', maxLength: 100,
  description: 'Slug of the project to act in, as koda_my_projects lists it' } as const;

export function withProjectArgument(d: KodaToolDescriptor): KodaToolDescriptor {
  const schema = d.inputSchema as { properties?: Record<string, unknown>; required?: string[] };
  return {
    ...d,
    description: `${d.description} In a workspace thread, name the project with \`project\` (a slug koda_my_projects lists).`,
    inputSchema: { ...d.inputSchema, properties: { project: PROJECT_ARGUMENT, ...(schema.properties ?? {}) },
      required: ['project', ...(schema.required ?? [])] },
  };
}

export function kodaToolDescriptorsFor(kind: ChatThreadKind): readonly KodaToolDescriptor[] {
  return kind === 'WORKSPACE' ? [KODA_MY_PROJECTS, ...KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS.map(withProjectArgument)] : KODA_TOOL_DESCRIPTORS;
}
```
`KODA_MY_PROJECTS` is a `KodaToolDescriptor` named `koda_my_projects` with `inputSchema` `{ type: 'object', properties: {}, additionalProperties: false }`; both live in `apps/api/src/fleet/assistant/koda-tool-descriptors.ts`.

**Goal** — Add the workspace-scoped `koda_my_projects` tool listing the creator's projects with open ticket, recent failed job and pending approval counts, and register it through a `WorkspaceTools` provider.

**Motivation** — The point of a WORKSPACE thread is questions across projects ("which projects have pending tickets?"); S5b-R12 requires `koda_my_projects` to list the creator's projects with those counts.

**Approach** — 1d2-D7 `koda_my_projects`. No input properties. The reachable projects are `ProjectsService.findAllForPrincipal(ctx.actor)` (non-deleted; member projects, or every project for a global ADMIN), ordered by slug ascending, the first 50. Per project, one line `<slug> open_tickets: <n> failed_jobs_7d: <n> pending_approvals: <n> /<slug>` and the project name under it through `ctx.data` (user-written text is fenced). Counts: open tickets = `Ticket` rows of the project with `deletedAt` null and `status` in `CREATED`, `VERIFIED`, `IN_PROGRESS`, `VERIFY_FIX`; failed jobs = `FleetJob` rows with `state` `FAILED` or `CRASHED` and `finishedAt` at or after `ctx.now` minus 7 days; pending approvals = `FleetApproval` rows with `status` `pending`. No reachable project -> `no projects`. More than 50 -> a last line `more: <n> projects not listed`. The counts come from one escape-hatch method, `findProjectCounts(projectIds, failedSince)`, on the existing `PrismaAssistantToolCallRepository` (which extends `AbstractPrismaRepository`, `.nax/rules/api-data.md:105-135`), exposed through a new interface `ProjectCountsReader` and token `PROJECT_COUNTS_READER` (`useExisting` the same class; both declared in `assistant-tool-call.domain.ts` next to `AssistantToolCallRepository`), as `findCallContext` is (1b-D6). It runs three `groupBy` reads keyed by `projectId` and answers a `Map<projectId, { openTickets, failedJobs7d, pendingApprovals }>` with zeros for projects that have no rows. `AssistantModule` imports `ProjectsModule` for `ProjectsService` (already in its graph through `MemoryModule`) and registers the `WorkspaceTools` provider, which registers `myProjectsTool` in `onModuleInit` like the other groups (mirror `tools/fleet-tools.ts`).
1d2-D5 (already implemented in US-002's executor; relied on here): a workspace-scoped tool in a WORKSPACE thread runs with no membership or ability check; its `ToolContext.projectId`/`projectSlug` are the home project's, which the tool must not read; `ctx.actor` is `userPrincipal(creator)` with no project role. 1d2-D6: the stored `AssistantToolCall.projectId` for such a call is the home project's id.
The tool descriptor served is `KODA_MY_PROJECTS` (created in US-001) and the tool's `scope` is `workspace`. `apps/api/src/common/test-helpers/global-stubs.module.ts` may gain a stub provider only if compiling `AssistantModule` with `ProjectsModule` needs one; no assertion changes.

**Scope** — In: `workspace-tools.ts`, `findProjectCounts`, `ProjectCountsReader`/`PROJECT_COUNTS_READER`, `AssistantModule` wiring, integration spec. Out: executor project-argument logic and route gate (US-002), descriptors (US-001), any write tool, pagination beyond 50.

**Interface**
```ts
// assistant-tool-call.domain.ts
export interface ProjectCounts { openTickets: number; failedJobs7d: number; pendingApprovals: number }
export interface ProjectCountsReader {
  findProjectCounts(projectIds: readonly string[], failedSince: Date): Promise<Map<string, ProjectCounts>>;
}
export const PROJECT_COUNTS_READER = Symbol('PROJECT_COUNTS_READER');
// workspace-tools.ts
export function myProjectsTool(/* ProjectsService, ProjectCountsReader */): AssistantTool; // scope: 'workspace', descriptor: KODA_MY_PROJECTS
```

**Goal** — Add the web-side building blocks for WORKSPACE threads: types, `eligibleModels` for WORKSPACE, a controller `threadPath` option, `useWorkspaceThreads`/`useWorkspaceThread` composables, create-body helpers, and the `/api/threads/:id/events` Nitro live-stream proxy.

**Motivation** — After 1d-1 the only way to use a WORKSPACE thread is the raw API; the pages (US-005, US-006) need a client over `/threads`, `/threads/:id`, `/threads/:id/messages` and `/threads/:id/events`.

**Approach** — Web routes call only routes 1d-1 shipped (`/threads`, `/threads/:id`, `/threads/:id/messages`, `/threads/:id/events`, `/projects`, `/projects/:slug/fleet/runners`). Changes:
- Web `ChatThreadKind` (`apps/web/lib/thread-types.ts:14`) becomes `'REPO' | 'PROJECT' | 'WORKSPACE'`. `ThreadDto` gains `homeProject?: { slug: string; name: string }`.
- Web `FleetRunnerSummary` (`apps/web/lib/fleet-types.ts:113`) gains `workspaceThreads: boolean` (the API already sends it, `runner-summary.dto.ts:53`).
- Web `eligibleModels(runners, kind)` (`apps/web/lib/thread-create.ts:97-102`): `WORKSPACE` keeps only runners with `workspaceThreads`; REPO and PROJECT unchanged. Native backends only (ACP is refused for WORKSPACE).
- Web `ThreadControllerDeps` (`apps/web/lib/thread-controller.ts:33-41`) gains optional `threadPath?: string`; when set it replaces the project path built at `:82`; `slug` stays for every existing caller.
- Web `server/utils/live-proxy.ts` gains `openWorkspaceThreadLiveUpstream(req: LiveUpstreamRequest & { apiInternalUrl: string; threadId: string })`, which refuses a thread id failing `THREAD_ID_PATTERN` with 400 `invalid thread id` and otherwise calls `openLiveUpstreamUrl` with `/threads/<id>/events` resolved by `resolveProxyTarget`.
- 1d2-D13 web live stream. A Nitro route `server/api/threads/[id]/events.get.ts` proxies `/threads/:id/events` with the cookie, mirroring the project thread route (`server/api/projects/[slug]/threads/[id]/events.get.ts`): an invalid thread id answers 400 without calling the API, and a non-200 upstream status passes through.
- `useWorkspaceThreads` (list: `load(status)` GET `/threads` with query `{ status, size: '100' }`, sets `threads` from `items` and `threadsEnabled` from the response) and `useWorkspaceThread(id)` (mirrors `composables/useThread.ts`, passing `threadPath: '/threads/<id>'` to `createThreadController` and opening its event stream through `createThreadEventStream` at `/api/threads/<id>/events`).
- `apps/web/lib/workspace-thread-create.ts`: `buildWorkspaceThreadCreateSchema(t)` (same `validation.required` message as `buildThreadCreateSchema`) and `toCreateWorkspaceThreadBody` returning `{ title, maxCostUsd, backend: { kind: 'native', model, effort? } }` plus `homeProject` only when non-empty (empty home project -> no `homeProject` key; empty effort -> no effort).
- Test fixtures typed `FleetRunnerSummary` (`tests/lib/thread-create.spec.ts:32`, `tests/lib/thread-create-project.spec.ts:20-30`, `tests/pages/threads-new-page.spec.ts`) gain `workspaceThreads: false` only if the web type-check rejects them; no assertion changes.

**Scope** — In: lib, composables, live proxy util, Nitro route, fixture type fixes. Out: any page, layout, palette, i18n or component change (US-005, US-006).

**Interface**
```ts
// lib/thread-types.ts
export type ChatThreadKind = 'REPO' | 'PROJECT' | 'WORKSPACE';
// ThreadDto: homeProject?: { slug: string; name: string }
// lib/thread-controller.ts
// ThreadControllerDeps: threadPath?: string
// server/utils/live-proxy.ts
export function openWorkspaceThreadLiveUpstream(req: LiveUpstreamRequest & { apiInternalUrl: string; threadId: string }): Promise<LiveUpstreamResult>;
// lib/workspace-thread-create.ts
export function buildWorkspaceThreadCreateSchema(t: (key: string) => string): /* schema, same style as buildThreadCreateSchema */;
export interface WorkspaceThreadCreateValues { homeProject: string; title: string; model: string; effort: string; maxCostUsd: number }
export function toCreateWorkspaceThreadBody(values: WorkspaceThreadCreateValues): CreateWorkspaceThreadBody;
// mirror the argument shape and return typing of toCreateThreadBody in lib/thread-create.ts
```

**Goal** — Add the `/threads/:id` detail page for a WORKSPACE thread with header, session controls, koda tools notice, timeline and composer, and a WORKSPACE branch in `ThreadHeader`.

**Motivation** — Without a detail page a WORKSPACE thread cannot be read or continued from the web.

**Approach** — 1d2-D12 web detail page. Header, session controls (end session, raise cap, archive), the koda tools notice, the timeline and the composer. No action bar (publish, plan, run), no stale banner and no project-code notice: WORKSPACE threads have none of those. The creator is the only possible viewer, so `isCreator` is true whenever the thread loads. Job cards link into the home project (`/<homeProject.slug>/fleet/jobs/<id>`), so the page passes `homeProject.slug` as the `slug` prop of `ThreadTimeline`. The header's runner summaries load only after the thread has loaded, from `GET /projects/<homeProject.slug>/fleet/runners` through a runners-only read (not `useFleetDispatchOptions`, whose `load()` also reads the repo list); no `/fleet/runners` request is made while the thread request is pending. The page loads the thread through `useWorkspaceThread(id)` (US-004; requests `/threads/<id>`, sends to `/threads/<id>/messages`, archive posts to `/threads/<id>/archive`), mirrors `pages/[project]/threads/[id].vue` for toasts (`extractApiError` on a failed send), the error state on a 404 load, and the tab title (prefix `• ` while a turn runs, via `isTurnRunning`/`sessionLabel`/`composerState` from `lib/thread-view.ts`). `ThreadHeader` (`components/fleet/threads/ThreadHeader.vue:21-23`): a WORKSPACE thread shows its home project name where a REPO thread shows `feat/<feature>`. `ThreadHeader`'s existing REPO and PROJECT label assertions do not change.

**Scope** — In: `pages/threads/[id].vue`, `ThreadHeader` WORKSPACE branch. Out: list/new pages, nav/palette (US-005), composables and live proxy (US-004), editing home project/title/backend after creation.

**Interface**
```ts
// pages/threads/[id].vue: route param id -> useWorkspaceThread(id)
// ThreadControls receives is-creator = true once the thread has loaded
// ThreadTimeline receives slug = thread.homeProject.slug
```
